# React Effects — Derive, Don't Set-State-In-Effect

## The rule

Next 16's eslint preset ships `react-hooks/set-state-in-effect`, which fires on patterns like:

```ts
// ❌ Lint error — react-hooks/set-state-in-effect
useEffect(() => {
  if (someCondition) setFoo([]);
}, [someCondition]);
```

When you find yourself reaching for `setState(...)` inside `useEffect` to "clear state on dep change" or "detect a client-only value at mount", **stop and rewrite as a render-time derivation** or `useSyncExternalStore`.

## Pattern A — Derive at render time

If the value you're trying to keep in state is fully a function of other state plus a discriminator, don't store it in state at all — derive it at render time:

```ts
// ❌ Wrong — clear pageComponents when activePageId changes
const [pageComponents, setPageComponents] = useState<{ forPageId: string; components: ComponentRecord[] } | null>(null);
useEffect(() => {
  if (!activePageId) setPageComponents(null);
}, [activePageId]);
const displayedComponents = pageComponents?.components ?? [];

// ✅ Right — keep the keyed state, project at render time
const [pageComponents, setPageComponents] = useState<{ forPageId: string; components: ComponentRecord[] } | null>(null);
const displayedComponents =
  pageComponents?.forPageId === activePageId ? pageComponents.components : [];
```

**Why:** state only changes when fetch resolves. "Clearing" is a render-time projection, not an effect side-effect. Fewer render cycles, no lint error, no stale-state bug when the dep changes mid-fetch.

This pattern is also recommended in the React 19 docs (https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect — "Adjusting some state when a prop changes").

## Pattern B — `useSyncExternalStore` for SSR-safe browser-only reads

If the value comes from `window`, `document`, `localStorage`, or any browser-only global, you cannot read it at render time directly without breaking the SSR contract. The obvious `useState(false)` + `useEffect(() => setEnabled(read()))` pattern trips the same lint rule.

The canonical Next 16 answer is `useSyncExternalStore` with a no-op subscribe:

```ts
// ❌ Wrong — set-state-in-effect lint error
const [debugEnabled, setDebugEnabled] = useState(false);
useEffect(() => {
  setDebugEnabled(new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get('debug') === '1');
}, []);

// ✅ Right — useSyncExternalStore handles SSR + client snapshot
const noopSubscribe = () => () => {};
const getClientSnapshot = () =>
  new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get('debug') === '1';
const getServerSnapshot = () => false;

function DebugPanel() {
  const debugEnabled = useSyncExternalStore(
    noopSubscribe,
    getClientSnapshot,
    getServerSnapshot,
  );
  if (!debugEnabled) return null;
  // ...
}
```

**Caveat (live-discovered):** both snapshot functions return a **referentially stable** value. Returning a fresh object/array each call triggers React's *"The result of getSnapshot should be cached to avoid an infinite loop"* error and cascades to *"Maximum update depth exceeded."* If the snapshot is computed (e.g. parsed JSON), cache the result at module scope or via a lazy getter.

```ts
// ❌ Wrong — fresh array every call, infinite loop
const getClientSnapshot = () => window.location.search.split('&');

// ✅ Right — module-level cache + lazy getter
let cached: readonly string[] | null = null;
const getClientSnapshot = () => {
  if (cached === null) cached = Object.freeze(window.location.search.split('&'));
  return cached;
};
```

For values that genuinely change after mount (resize events, focus, network state), pair `useSyncExternalStore` with a real subscribe function instead of the no-op.

## When set-state-in-effect IS still correct

Two narrow cases remain legitimate:

1. **Reacting to async work that resolves:** the effect kicks off a fetch / subscribe and the resolution callback updates state. The state update happens in the `.then(...)` or `onSuccess(...)` handler, NOT directly in the effect body. The lint rule allows this.
2. **Imperative integration with a non-React library:** mounting a chart, attaching a third-party listener, etc. — the effect's job is the imperative call; state updates flow through whatever callback the library provides.

Both are async + callback-driven. The lint rule fires on **synchronous** `setState(derivedValue)` calls that could be replaced by render-time derivation or `useSyncExternalStore`.

